  "exception": {
    "type": "no-defensible-ucr-match",
    "checkedOn": "2026-10-12",
    "reason": "Three independently organised directions were tested. Historical and analytical musicology has isolated UCR material on modern history, art history, rhetoric and cultural interpretation but no music history, music theory, aural study, score-based analysis or sustained criticism of musical works. Music, society and cultural institutions has genuine sociology, politics, qualitative research, marketing and museum-study analogues, but those courses study general social and cultural systems rather than music as their recurring object. Music and media has film, media archaeology, digital culture, documentary and creative communication, including limited work with sound, but no cumulative study of music in screen media, recording, interactive media, music software or music criticism. A 24-course curriculum in any direction would therefore become a general media, sociology, communication or cultural-studies programme. The missing musical object, historical sequence and analytical formation are academic content rather than professional accreditation requirements, so no complete UCR response is claimed.",
    "curriculumContext": "The selected Music and Society pathway closes 60+60+60: 60 EC of foundations, 60 EC of major context/depth/preparation/thesis requirements and 60 EC genuine profiling. The first-listed 30-EC Music and Society package and first-listed 7.5-EC context option are neutral bounded choices made independently of UCR fit. The approved 30-EC profile package and remaining 30 EC of free profiling remain generic. Formal instruction is Dutch plus English.",
    "ucrCourseEvidence": "Reviewed all 151 rows in the current enriched UCR workbook, including course descriptions or outline-derived profiles, prerequisites and Fall 2026-start availability. MCCMEDI111 and MCCLITR311 analyse film form and include sound or music as one element of audiovisual texts; MCCMEDI112 studies pre-digital media such as cassette tapes and the Sony Walkman; MCCMEDI311 considers music among several cultural forms in a course centred on race and power; MCCMEDI312 includes audio production within documentary practice; and MCCCOMM312 permits sound-based conceptual work but explicitly excludes singing, songwriting and music performance. GOSSOCI111, GOSSOCI212, GOSSOCI214 and GOSSOCI311 provide social theory and qualitative research, while MCCHEAM111, MCCHEAM312 and MCCHEAM316 provide art-history, institutions and curatorial research. These are genuine adjacent contributions, but no workbook course teaches music theory, notation, harmony or form; European or global music history; close musical analysis and critical listening; musicological methods; screen-music, popular-music or recording history as sustained subjects; or an advanced musicology research sequence. Academic feasibility fails before course scheduling, so no 24-course programme or UCR-credit comparison is fabricated.",
